WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 31 Leader Video
Unit 8, Lesson 3: Doubt

1
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.470
So years ago, when I was a high school student, I walked away from Christianity.

2
00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.170
And I went down a whole other path for a good long time.

3
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:21.150
The primary reason that I walked away is because

4
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.590
I had questions and I had doubts.

5
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:30.190
And you've gotten up to this point now where not only have you all this content

6
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.250
and we've talked about love, but we've talked about the power of questions and the

7
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.110
power of conversation.

8
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.730
But somewhere in there at some point, questions from others,

9
00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:47.610
information from other people, going to college, all kinds of different things are

10
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.430
going to be coming at your students, causing doubt.

11
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.790
What happens when they doubt? See, I walked away because I thought, well, because I

12
00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.850
was doubting and I asked a question, I was told not to ask the questions.

13
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:07.430
I was told to just believe. I was told to just have faith and I didn't know what

14
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:13.210
that even meant and I just couldn't figure it all out so I just said enough

15
00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:14.610
and I walk away.

16
00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:22.690
The statistics are still out there and they're still pretty prevalent that a

17
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:28.050
large majority of students leave youth group after high school and there's a whole

18
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.130
bunch of reasons for that.

19
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.510
One of them is because when they have doubts, they're not getting answered.

20
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:41.410
So this is an opportunity to open it up to your students and say, listen, I get it.

21
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.710
I know you have doubts. Maybe share some of your own and that's okay.

22
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.590
Let's talk about them. Let's make sure this is an open forum.

23
00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.310
We talked weeks ago, the very beginning of this process, the idea of having an open

24
00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:58.250
communication and having a youth group that was very authentic and very open

25
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.230
and invited the difficult questions.

26
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.650
And we talked about that those difficult questions were going to come.

27
00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:11.330
They're probably going to come even more now, because sometimes the more information

28
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.970
that you have, the more questions you have as a result.

29
00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.230
So invite those in. Invite those doubts.

30
00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.830
If students are not sure about Jesus, not sure about the existence of God, not sure

31
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.370
about the biblical text, not sure about evil and pain and suffering, not sure

32
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.890
about any of the things we've talked about and maybe some things we haven't talked

33
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.490
about. Because there's a whole bunch of cultural issues that step into this that you

34
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.630
have to help students navigate. And that's where helping students navigate doubt

35
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:44.490
is an incredibly important thing and an important tool at actually furthering their

36
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.730
faith. Because as they get those things answered, as they feel free to

37
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:55.650
express those doubts, that tells them that as leaders,

38
00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.030
we understand and that we've been there and that it's okay and we're going to walk

39
00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.590
with you through those. So for the introduction activity this week, we asked

40
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.230
students literally to write down one question that they've been wrestling with.

41
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:14.830
Now, you might, as a large group activity, you might actually take and compile some

42
00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.310
of those. You might do a Q&A session.

43
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.310
I find Q&A sessions to be incredibly helpful.

44
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.170
I open up the floor to students and just let them ask whatever they want.

45
00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.910
And yeah, you get some funny ones.

46
00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:31.590
I get a lot of dinosaur questions and some interesting ones about Adam and Eve.

47
00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.790
And those can be fun. You can play around with those a little bit and have some fun

48
00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:42.050
with it. But at the same time, some really good and profound questions will come up.

49
00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:47.350
And the advantage to that is that if one student asks a question and another

50
00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:51.570
student's afraid to ask the same question, the student that was afraid gets their

51
00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.530
question answered. And that can be an incredibly important and powerful tool.

52
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.910
So I would encourage you to maybe do that this week or find a way to incorporate

53
00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.730
many questions into your large group time.

54
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.790
And then during small group time, you can drill those questions down a little bit

55
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:12.130
deeper. But make sure as you do that that your small group leaders are adequately

56
00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.010
prepared to handle some of the questions.

57
00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.390
Based on your situation, the leadership that you have in place, and the volunteers

58
00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:24.550
that you have in place, just make sure that they are equipped to properly answer.

59
00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:30.450
And remind them that it's okay that they don't have an answer to say, I don't have

60
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:35.950
an answer. And to write those questions down and bring them back and find a way to

61
00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.570
get those questions answered specifically.

62
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:44.230
Another important, powerful tool is your storyteller this week to

63
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:50.050
have somebody in front of your students that has actually expressed doubt and walked

64
00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:54.810
through it in some way shape or form can be incredibly important that story whatever

65
00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:00.050
they've been through will resonate I'm sure with many of your students so make sure

66
00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.890
that you don't need to find the best story or the biggest story or the most

67
00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:08.250
compelling story but just so that students can see that hey I'm not the only one

68
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.390
going through this and I'm I'm not the only one that has, I'm not the only student

69
00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.730
dealing with this and I'm not the only person that's ever dealt with this and people

70
00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:18.370
have got through it because some students might just have a little couple of

71
00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.810
questions here and there. Other students might be at a significant crisis of faith

72
00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.610
in their lives where they need serious direction.

73
00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.930
And after all that you've gone through in this study, some of those things may start

74
00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.890
to be, you know, coming up to the surface a little bit.

75
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.590
So remind your students

76
00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:40.030
that no matter their questions, no matter the doubts, God can handle it

77
00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:46.010
and that he has given us everything we need to be entirely confident of who

78
00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.410
he is, who Jesus is, and what our salvation means.

